ite group and $\\mathrm{Irr}(G)$ be the set of all complex irreducible characters of $G$. The character-graph $\\Delta(G)$ associated to $G$, is a graph whose vertex set is the set of primes which divide the degrees of some characters in $\\mathrm{Irr}(G)$ and two distinct primes $p$ and $q$ are adjacent in $\\Delta(G)$ if the product $pq$ divides $\\chi(1)$, for some $\\chi\\in\\mathrm{Irr}(G)$. Tong-viet posed the conjecture that if $\\Delta(G)$ is $k$-regular for some integer $k\\geqslant 2$, then $\\Delta(G)$ is either a complete graph or a cocktail party graph. In this paper, we show that his conjecture is true for all regular character-graphs whose eigenvalues are in the interval $[-2, \\infty )$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPseudocompact and countably compact group topologies on Abelian groups 391 (https://www.math.sci.ehime-u.ac.jp/~dima/papers/opit2/structure_theory.pdf)\nciteturn7search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; The next question provides a slightly less bold conjecture:\n\n**Question 11.** Is it true that an Abelian group \\(G\\) belongs to **C** if and only if \\(G\\) satisfies both PS and CC? 894?\n\n**Question 12 ([22]).** Is it true in ZFC that an Abelian group \\(G\\) of size at most \\(2^{\\mathfrak{c}}\\) belongs to **C** if and only if \\(G\\) satisfies both PS and CC? 895?\n\nQuestion 12 has a positive consistent answer [22]. Assuming MA, there exist countably compact Abelian groups \\(G, H\\) such that \\(G \\times H\\) is not countably compact [31]. Therefore, our next question could be viewed as a weaker form of productivity of countable compactness in topological groups that still has a chance for a positive answer in ZFC.\n\n**Question 13 ([19]).** If \\(G\\) and \\(H\\) belong to **C**, must then their product \\(G \\times H\\) also belong to **C**? 896?\n\nIn fact, one can consider a much bolder hypothesis:\n\n**Question 14 ([19]).** Is **C** closed under arbitrary products? That is, if \\(G_i\\) belongs to **C** for each \\(i \\in I\\), does then \\(\\prod_{i \\in I} G_i\\) belong to **C**? 897?\n\nThe next question provides a slightly less bold conjecture:\n\n**Question 15 ([19]).** (i) Is there a cardinal \\(\\tau\\) having the following property: A product \\(\\prod_{i \\in I} G_i\\) belongs to **C** provided that \\(\\prod_{j \\in J} G_j\\) belongs to **C** whenever \\(J \\subseteq I\\) and \\(|J| \\le \\tau\\)?\n\n(ii) Does the statement in item (i) hold true when \\(\\tau = \\mathfrak{c}\\) or \\(\\tau = 2^{\\mathfrak{c}}\\)? 898–899?\n\nOf course Question 14 simply asks if the statement in item (i) of Question 15 holds true when \\(\\tau = 1\\). It might be worth noting that Question 15 is motivated by a theorem of Ginsburg and Saks [35]: A product \\(\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i\\) of topological spaces \\(X_i\\) is countably compact provided that \\(\\prod_{j \\in J} G_j\\) is countably compact whenever \\(J \\subseteq I\\) and \\(|J| \\le 2^{\\mathfrak{c}}\\).\n\nA partial positive answer to Question 14 has been given in [20]: It is consistent with ZFC that, for every family \\(\\{G_i : i \\in I\\}\\) of groups with \\(2^{|I|} \\le 2^{\\mathfrak{c}}\\) such that--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDimensions of the irreducible representations of the symmetric and alternating group (https://cage.ugent.be/~kdbaene/Homepage_files/Representations.pdf)\nciteturn7search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.8 years ago; This proves a conjecture by Tong-Viet. The main ingredient in the proof is a result on large primefactors in short intervals. ... ** *Let \\(G\\) be a finite group and \\(H\\) be a finite nonabelian simple group such that the sets of character degrees of \\(G\\) and \\(H\\) are the same.\nDimensions of the irreducible representations of the symmetric and alternating group\n\nKorneel Debaene\n\n**Abstract**\n\nWe establish the existence of an irreducible representation of \\(A_n\\) whose dimension does not occur as the dimension of an irreducible representation of \\(S_n\\), and vice versa. This proves a conjecture by Tong-Viet. The main ingredient in the proof is a result on large primefactors in short intervals.\n\n# 1 Introduction and statement of results\n\nTo what extent are groups determined by their characters? On the one hand, there are examples known of non-isomorphic groups with exactly the same character table, e.g. \\(Q_8\\) and \\(D_8\\). On the other hand, for nonabelian simple groups, a conjecture by Huppert predicts quite a different behaviour.\n\n**Conjecture.** *Let \\(G\\) be a finite group and \\(H\\) be a finite nonabelian simple group such that the sets of character degrees of \\(G\\) and \\(H\\) are the same. Then there exists an abelian group \\(A\\) such that \\(G \\cong H \\times A\\).*\n\nThis conjecture has been verified for many simple groups, but remains open for the alternating groups \\(A_n\\) when \\(n \\ge 14\\).\n\nIn this regard, Tong-Viet proved that the *multiset* of dimensions of irreducible representations of \\(A_n\\) determines \\(A_n\\) in [8]. He conjectured that the set of dimensions of irreducible representations is different for \\(A_n\\) and \\(S_n\\) in [7], and proved it for the special case that \\(n\\) is of the form \\(2p+1\\).\n\nWe will prove the following theorem, which proves Tong-Viets conjecture, and gives some indication in favour of Huppert’s Conjecture.\n\n**Theorem 1.1.** *The set \\(\\{\\dim \\rho \\mid \\rho\\) irreducible representation of \\(S_n\\}\\) is not equal to the set \\(\\{\\dim \\rho \\mid \\rho\\) irreducible representation of \\(A_n\\}\\), for all \\(n \\ge 3\\).*\n\nIn fact, the proof is constructive and gives a specific irreducible representation of \\(S_n\\) whose dimension does not occur as a dimension of any irreducible representation of\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFinite groups whose prime graphs are regular (https://arxiv.org/abs/1307.2175)\nciteturn7academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.3 years ago; Tong-Viet ... The prime graph \\Delta(G) associated to G is a graph whose vertex set is \\rho(G) and there is an edge between two distinct primes p and q if and only if the product pq divides some character degree of G. In this paper, we show that the prime graph \\Delta(G) of a finite group G is 3-regular if and only if it is a complete graph with four vertices.\nTitle: Finite groups whose prime graphs are regular\nAuthors: Hung P. Tong-Viet\nDate: Fri Jul  5 15:16:08 2013\n\nLet G be a finite group and let Irr(G) be the set of all irreducible complex characters of G. Let cd(G) be the set of all character degrees of G and denote by \\rho(G) the set of primes which divide some character degrees of G. The prime graph \\Delta(G) associated to G is a graph whose vertex set is \\rho(G) and there is an edge between two distinct primes p and q if and only if the product pq divides some character degree of G. In this paper, we show that the prime graph \\Delta(G) of a finite group G is 3-regular if and only if it is a complete graph with four vertices.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nON THE CHARACTER DEGREE GRAPH (https://air.unimi.it/retrieve/dfa8b999-a51b-748b-e053-3a05fe0a3a96/BipartiteComplement_revised.pdf)\nciteturn7search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.4 years ago; Tong-Viet, and provides some evidence for the famous \\(\\rho\\)-\\(\\sigma\\) conjecture by B. ... The character degree graph \\(\\Delta(G)\\) is thus defined as the (simple undirected) graph whose vertex set is the set \\(V(G)\\) of all the prime numbers that divide some \\(\\chi(1)\\in \\mathrm{cd}(G)\\), while a pair \\(\\{p,q\\}\\) of distinct vertices \\(p\\) and \\(q\\), belongs to the edge set \\(E(G)\\) if and only if \\(pq\\) divides an element in \\(\\mathrm{cd}(G)\\).\n\nThere is a well-developed literature on character degree graphs (see for instance the survey [6]).\nON THE CHARACTER DEGREE GRAPH  \nOF SOLVABLE GROUPS\n\nZEINAB AKHLAGHI, CARLO CASOLO, SILVIO DOLFI, KHATOON KHEDRI,  \nAND EMANUELE PACIFICI\n\n**Abstract.** Let \\(G\\) be a finite solvable group, and let \\(\\Delta(G)\\) denote the *prime graph* built on the set of degrees of the irreducible complex characters of \\(G\\). A fundamental result by P.P. Pálfy asserts that the complement \\(\\overline{\\Delta}(G)\\) of the graph \\(\\Delta(G)\\) does not contain any cycle of length 3. In this paper we generalize Pálfy’s result, showing that \\(\\overline{\\Delta}(G)\\) does not contain any cycle of odd length, whence it is a bipartite graph. As an immediate consequence, the set of vertices of \\(\\Delta(G)\\) can be covered by two subsets, each inducing a complete subgraph. The latter property yields in turn that if \\(n\\) is the clique number of \\(\\Delta(G)\\), then \\(\\Delta(G)\\) has at most \\(2n\\) vertices. This confirms a conjecture by Z. Akhlaghi and H.P. Tong-Viet, and provides some evidence for the famous \\(\\rho\\)-\\(\\sigma\\) conjecture by B. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHung Tong-Viet - Publications (https://sites.google.com/binghamton.edu/tongviet/publications)\nciteturn7search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Hung Tong-Viet ... Schaeffer Fry), Bulletin of the London Mathematical Society 58 (2026), no. 7, paper no. e70428, 27pp\n\nHung Tong-Viet\n\n# Publications\n\n### \n\n Here are some of my recent publications. A complete list of my publications can be found in MathScinet. Some of my papers are available on arXiv.\n\n2026\n\n  1. Sylow subgroups and the number of irreducible characters of degrees divisible by a prime p (with J. P. Cossey, M. L. Lewis, A. A. Schaeffer Fry), Bulletin of the London Mathematical Society 58 (2026), no. 7, paper no. e70428, 27pp\n\n  2. On the invariants of finite groups arising in a topological quantum field theory (with C